    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$3.11 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a Korean-style pork bowl that would run $12 or more from a takeout counter. That is nearly two weeks of dinners from one skillet.",
    "shop_smart":  "Ground pork is the whole reason this bowl is so cheap. It routinely undercuts ground beef by a solid margin per pound with the same job description.\nGochujang, mirin, sesame oil, and soy sauce all live in the pantry for months. The first trip stings a little, then every batch after this one costs mostly just the pork.\nFresh ginger freezes whole. Buy the two pieces, use what you need, and grate the rest straight from the freezer next time instead of buying again.",
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cook the rice: 2 cups dry with water per your usual method.",
                    "Stir the sauce together: gochujang, mirin, sugar, soy sauce, and half the sesame oil.",
                    "Brown the ground pork in your big pot over medium-high heat, breaking it up, until no pink remains. Drain if needed, then season with the salt and black pepper.",
                    "Add the diced onion, garlic, and ginger and cook 3 to 4 minutes until fragrant.",
                    "Pour in the sauce and simmer 5 minutes until it glazes the pork and thickens slightly.",
                    "Finish with the rest of the sesame oil, most of the green onions, and the toasted sesame seeds. Serve over the rice with the last of the green onions on top."
                ],
